d each other's backs and we  kept each other going the crash happened  close enough to the ship for some  sailors to witness but the frigate  continued on with this mission with the  same crew  for another three months before finally  turning for home  we embarked a padre  we embarked two social workers and  executed a very robust plan  of interviewing the ship's company and  affording them access to those mental  health  resources for three months families of  the deceased have been reflecting on the  lives that were lost  some lieutenant abigail cobra's father  who himself serves in the navy  says his daughter loved adventure riding  motorcycles was a  passion and a hobby watching her player  bagpipes  would be something i had to miss but  then there's the more personal items you  know walking her down the aisle when she  decides to get married  first child those types of things that  just  won't ever happen  for others a long absence means a long  embrace  even if emotional reunions were moved to  a separate location to avoid  overcrowding  we've all been working together in our  own little quarantine world right and  now now we're back in the real world  it's been a long six months yeah it's uh  with covet hitting and everything else  and not having family around  it's been it's been a challenge but he's  home  hopefully for a long time now still  making adjustments but  at least they're home ross lord global  news  halifax  scholarship or stunt still ahead how  facebook is partnering with a canadian  university to fight hate  online  an ontario teenager has pleaded guilty  to terrorism charges relating to a bomb  plot in the name of the so-called  islamic state  the 17 year old originally from syria  was arrested in january of last year he  was 16 at the time and can't be  identified because he's still a minor  the plot involved a plan to use a  pressure cooker  bombed to target bars and nightclubs as  well as members of the police and the  military  canadian authorities learned of the plot  after an undercover informant  tipped off the fbi an ontario university  has teamed up with facebook to try to  stop the spread of hate online  ontario's tech university is tasked by  facebook canada to spot online hate and  misinformation  and to develop strategies and tools to  counter it the university's global  network against hate will receive five  hundred thousand dollars over five years  professor barbara perry says it's a good  start but that facebook  has a long way to go  you know are they doing enough no  they're absolutely not doing enough we  will continue to  to push and i think that's part of what  this relationship is about is  you know providing the information that  uh  facebook needs to move forward and  continuing to to apply pressure  uh and to urge them to make the kinds of  changes that need to be made  uh in terms of both their human capacity  and their technological capacity for  responding  dangerous misinformation still gets  shared millions of times on social media  often  unchecked today facebook twitter and  youtube did remove a video but only  after it went viral  it shows a group of people in lab coats  who call themselves america's  front line doctors because there's a lot  of inaccuracies with the testing  and also even if the test the video  published by right-wing media outlet  breitbart news was shared millions of  times including by president trump  before it was taken down  it claimed falsely that the anti-malaria  drug hydroxychloroquine is a cure for  covid  and that you don't need a mask to slow  the spread of the novel coronavirus next  an orca nbc gives scientists something  to celebrate after a heartbreaking loss  [Music]  russian scientists are digging to find  the rest of this woolly mammoth skeleton  locals discovered the bones near a  siberian lake some parts still have soft  tissue intact which is remarkable  because the woolly mammoths went extinct  some 10 000 years ago  there's been a dramatic rise in  temperature in siberia recently that's  melting the permafrost  and revealing these ancient skeletons  and you may remember the orca j-35 she  was the endangered southern resident  killer whale who  two years ago carried her dead calf for  17 days  it's a story that show the powerful bond  between a mother and baby  as jennifer palmer reports that orca is  now pregnant again  and she's not the only one it's a  picture that's led to a lot of  excitement an  indication an endangered family is  getting ready to grow  we're very excited about it j-35 also  known as telequa  is the mother orca who made  international headlines in 2018 when she  carried her dead calf around the ocean  for 17 days  traveling more than 1600 kilometers  there's been interest in them for 30  and 40 years dr lance barrett leonard  director of the marine mammal research  program with oceanwise  says it's not unusual for orcas to do  this for a short period of time but  j-35's apparent tour of grief shows her  emotional attachment to her calf  j-35 is now 22 years old she also has  another calf  the one she's carrying now is expected  to arrive next year  they have suffered unusually high  mortality in the last few years  and so they've got to make it up with  reproduction so  you know every calf that's born and  there's only been a trickle of them over  the last few years we  watch with great interest researchers  are keeping a keen  eye on southern resident killer whales  because their population is in a  critical state  there's only 73 of them remaining and  their gestation periods are  long lasting up to 17 months so every  pregnancy  is important scientists say there are  three things  threatening them contaminants underwater  vessel noise and lack of food in  particular  chinook salmon they struggle to get  enough food  and we know from the collaborative work  done between the canadian  researchers myself and my colleagues in  the u.s  that the southern residents are  consistently thin but there's another  glimmer of hope  researchers have found another orca l-72  who is also expecting  both pregnancies being watched carefully  not just by researchers  but by their own pods calves and mothers  spend their lives  together it's a testimony really to how  resilient they are that they're that  they're still around now  jennifer palma global news vancouver  and that is global national for this  tuesday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is stanley nova scotia about an  hour north of halifax beautiful sunset  we'd love to see your canada please  email it to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

